          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            = succeed "," do
              %strong 2-category equipped with proarrows
            %em proarrow equipment
            or simply an
            = succeed "," do
              %em equipment
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            together with extra “proarrows” which enable one to reproduce more category theory inside it than is possible in a general 2-category. The ur-example is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            equipped with
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profunctors"} profunctors
          %p
            See also
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/framed+bicategory"} framed bicategory
          %h2#motivation Motivation
          %p
            Just as ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            provides a framework in which one can do “formal mathematics,” one of the (many) purposes of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            is to provide a framework in which one can do
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+category+theory"} formal category theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunctions"} adjunctions
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads"} monads
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+fibrations"} Grothendieck fibrations
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+extensions"} Kan extensions
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+faithful+morphisms"} fully faithful morphisms
          %p
            However, these internalizations are not always “correct” in every 2-category. For instance, in the 2-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V Cat
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+categories"} categories enriched
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "-adjunction" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "-monad," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "-functor" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "-fully-faithfulness." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V Cat
          %p
            Furthermore, some concepts of category theory are difficult to interpret at all in a 2-category. The notion of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
            in ordinary category theory can be interpreted in a 2-category by identifying the limit of a functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D\to C
            as its Kan extension along the unique functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo →
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D\to *
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weighted+limit"} weighted limit
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V Cat
          %p
            What is missing is that the 2-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V Cat
            doesn’t natively supply any information about the
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            these hom-functors can be recovered by looking at
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comma+categories"} comma categories
            = succeed "\u2014in" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comma+objects"} comma objects
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profunctor"} profunctor
          %p
            There are several not-quite-equivalent ways to describe this extra structure. One due to Street and Walters, called a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+structure"} Yoneda structure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            a “presheaf object”
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P A
            and a “Yoneda arrow”
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\to P A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\to B
            is then identified with an arrow
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \to P A
            = succeed "," do
              %em proarrow equipment
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V Cat
          %h2#Definitions Definitions
          %p There are several equivalent ways to define proarrow equipments on a 2-category.
          %h3#DefinitionAsA2Functor Definition as a 2-functor
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            be a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                equip
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
                with proarrows
          %ul
            %li
              A 2-category
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              = succeed "." do
                %em proarrows
            %li
              A functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo →
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K\to M
              which is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijective-on-objects+functor"} bijective on objects
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+fully+faithful+2-functor"} locally fully faithful
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon A\to B
              is a 1-morphism in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              as
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ↛
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(1,f)\colon A\nrightarrow B
            %li
              For each arrow
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon A\to B
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(1,f)\colon A\to B
              has a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(f,1)
          %p
            As usual on the nLab, here by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            we mean a weak 2-category (aka
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            we mean a weak 2-functor (aka
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudofunctor"} pseudofunctor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            is in fact a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-category"} strict 2-category
          %p
            For a proarrow
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H\colon B\to D
            and ordinary arrows
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon A\to B
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g\colon C\to D
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi g
                  %mo ,
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H(g,f)
            for the composite
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi H
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(g,1) \circ  H \circ B(1,f)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_A
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A(1,1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            for the identity proarrow
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ↛
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\nrightarrow A
          %p
            Note that in the case where proarrows are profunctors,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi g
                  %mo ,
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H(g, f)
            is not the action on a set of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heteromorphisms"} heteromorphisms
            by pre- and post- composing with morphisms; instead it is the functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %h4#terminology Terminology
          %p
            We refer to the entire structure defined above as a
            %strong 2-category equipped with proarrows
            or a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong 2-category proarrow equipment
            %strong proarrow equipment
            or simply
            %strong equipment
            for the entire structure. We prefer “2-category equipped with proarrows” (or, equivalently, “pro-morphisms”) for the following reasons:
          %ul
            %li
              It conveys that we are talking about extra stuff added to a 2-category. (Actually, it is “eka-stuff” or “2-stuff” in the sense of
              = succeed ".)" do
                %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/stuff,+structure,+property"} stuff, structure, property
            %li
              It includes the word “proarrow” which tells people what the extra stuff consists of, namely a generalization of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profunctors"} profunctors
            %li It includes the word “equipment” which signals what is meant to readers who are familiar with that term.
          %p We do sometimes avail ourselves of the shorter terminology “proarrow equipment,” however, once we are clear what is under discussion.
          %h4#examples Examples
          %p
            For example, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            is a (Benabou)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmos"} cosmos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo =
                  %mi V
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K= V Cat
            of
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mo =
                  %mi V
                  %mi Mod
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M=V Mod
            of
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(1,f)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(f,1)
            are the two ways of regarding a
            = succeed "-functor" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:A\to B
            as a profunctor, namely
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi f
                  %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(-,f-)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(f-,-)
            (hence the notation in the general case). Composition of
            = succeed "-profunctors" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coends"} coends
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            to be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocomplete+category"} cocomplete
            with colimits preserved by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊗
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
            on both sides in order to form such associative tensor products. In
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V Cat
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi g
                  %mo ,
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H(g,f)
            is the result of precomposing the profunctor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi op
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H:D^{op}\otimes B \to V
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi g
                      %mi op
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g^{op}\otimes f
          %p
            The other most common sorts of generalized category, such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+categories"} internal categories
            in some category with pullbacks, and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibered+categories"} fibered categories
            over some base, are also naturally equipped with proarrows. For internal categories in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            to have finite limits and coequalizers preserved by pullback in order for the bicategory of internal profunctors to have associative compositions. (See “virtual equipments,” below, for a context which avoids some of these restrictions on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            and
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
          %h3#DefinitionAsDoubleCategory Definition as a double category
          %p
            We discuss now how a 2-category with proarrow equipment is equivalently a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+category"} double category
            with extra structure.
          %p
            Given a 2-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            equipped with proarrows, we can construct a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+category"} double category
            having the same objects as
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi H
                        %mtd
                          %mi C
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo
                            %mi f
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⇓
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi g
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mi J
                        %mtd
                          %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{A &amp; \overset{H}{\to} &amp; C \\
                  ^f\downarrow &amp; \Downarrow &amp; \downarrow^g\\
                  B&amp; \underset{J}{\to} &amp; D}
          %p
            are the 2-cells
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mo →
                    %mi J
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo ,
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H \to J(g,f)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            is a strict 2-category, then this is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo+double+category"} pseudo double category
            (vertically strict and horizontally weak) while if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            are both weak 2-categories, this double category is weak in both directions (like a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+bicategory"} double bicategory
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V Cat
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi J
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi g
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mi f
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H(b,a) \to J(g b,f a)
            natural in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mi Cat
                    %mo →
                    %mi V
                    %mi Prof
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V Cat \to V Prof
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            is any 2-category equipped with proarrows, we can reconstruct
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            and its proarrow equipment from the double category defined above, and we can characterize the double categories that arise in this way.
          %p
            One way to state this characterization is that they are those in which every vertical 1-cell
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon A\to B
            has both a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/companion"} companion
            (namely
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(1,f)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conjoint"} conjoint
            (namely
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(f,1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            as the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertical+2-category"} vertical and horizontal 2-categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo →
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K\to M
            as the functor taking every vertical arrow to its companion. Whenever a vertical arrow has both a companion
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(1,f)
            and a conjoint
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(f,1)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(1,f)\dashv B(f,1)
          %p Another, perhaps even more natural, way to characterize these double categories is as those with the following property: given any “niche”
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo
                            %mi f
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi g
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi C
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo ↛
                            %mi J
                        %mtd
                          %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{A &amp; &amp; B\\
                  ^f\downarrow &amp; &amp; \downarrow^g\\
                  C&amp; \underset{J}{\nrightarrow} &amp; D}
          %p there exists a “universal” or “cartesian” filler square
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mi J
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi g
                              %mo ,
                              %mi f
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo
                            %mi f
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⇓
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi g
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi C
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo ↛
                            %mi J
                        %mtd
                          %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{A &amp; \overset{J(g,f)}{\to} &amp; B\\
                  ^f\downarrow &amp; \Downarrow &amp; \downarrow^g\\
                  C&amp; \underset{J}{\nrightarrow} &amp; D}
          %p with the property that any other square
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↛
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo
                            %mrow
                              %mi f
                              %mi h
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⇓
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mrow
                              %mi g
                              %mi k
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi C
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo ↛
                            %mi J
                        %mtd
                          %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{X &amp; \nrightarrow &amp; Y\\
                  ^{f h}\downarrow &amp; \Downarrow &amp; \downarrow^{g k}\\
                  C&amp; \underset{J}{\nrightarrow} &amp; D}
          %p factors through the universal one uniquely:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↛
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo
                            %mi h
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %mo ∃
                          %mo !
                          %mo ⇓
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi k
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mi J
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi g
                              %mo ,
                              %mi f
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo
                            %mi f
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⇓
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi g
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi C
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo ↛
                            %mi J
                        %mtd
                          %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{X &amp; \nrightarrow &amp; Y\\
                  ^{h}\downarrow &amp; \exists! \Downarrow  &amp; \downarrow^{k}\\
                  A &amp; \overset{J(g,f)}{\to} &amp; B\\
                  ^f\downarrow &amp; \Downarrow &amp; \downarrow^g\\
                  C&amp; \underset{J}{\nrightarrow} &amp; D}
          %p
            The profunctor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi g
                  %mo ,
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J(g,f)
            will, of course, turn out to be the same one we gave that name to earlier. The proarrows
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mi B
                  %mo ,
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(1,f)=U_B(id_B,f)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(f,1) = U_B(f,id_B)
            are then a special case of this construction. We show that they are a companion and conjoint of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
          %p By factoring the identity square
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi U
                                %mi A
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo
                            %mi f
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi U
                                %mi f
                          %mo ⇓
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi f
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi U
                                %mi B
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{A &amp; \overset{U_A}{\to} &amp; A\\
                  ^f\downarrow &amp; ^{U_f}\Downarrow &amp; \downarrow^f\\
                  B  &amp; \underset{U_B}{\to} &amp; B}
          %p through the universal fillers
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mi B
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mn 1
                              %mo ,
                              %mi f
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo
                            %mi f
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⇓
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi id
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi U
                                %mi B
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mtext and
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mi B
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi f
                              %mo ,
                              %mn 1
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo
                            %mi id
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⇓
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi f
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi U
                                %mi B
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{A &amp; \overset{B(1,f)}{\to} &amp; B\\
                  ^f\downarrow &amp;\Downarrow &amp; \downarrow^{id}\\
                  B &amp; \underset{U_B}{\to} &amp; B} \qquad\text{and}\qquad
                  \\array{B &amp; \overset{B(f,1)}{\to} &amp; A\\
                  ^{id}\downarrow &amp;\Downarrow &amp; \downarrow^{f}\\
                  B &amp; \underset{U_B}{\to} &amp; B}
          %p
            that define
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(1,f)
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(f,1)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi U
                                %mi A
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo
                            %mi f
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⇓
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi id
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mi B
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi f
                              %mo ,
                              %mn 1
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
